  "sub": "Physical Database Design",
  "q": "By creating a foreign key constraint, the following are usually considered during design -",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Delete and update cascading",
   "B": "Size of database and growth estimation",
   "C": "Skill and competency of database administrators",
   "D": "Whether web-based or application-based"
  },
  "ans": "A",
  "exp": "When defining a foreign key, the designer must specify referential-integrity actions such as ON DELETE CASCADE/SET NULL/RESTRICT and ON UPDATE CASCADE, dictating what happens to child rows when the referenced parent row is deleted or its key changes. (B) database sizing/growth is a capacity-planning concern unrelated to the FK's referential-action semantics. (C) DBA skill level is an organizational/staffing matter, not a schema design decision. (D) deployment architecture (web vs desktop) has no bearing on how a foreign key constraint itself is defined.",
  "prov": "Official MPSC final answer key",
