  "q": "Which of the following is a kind of stored procedure that is executed as a side-effect of some operations on the data?",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Commit",
   "B": "Function",
   "C": "Alert",
   "D": "Trigger"
  },
  "ans": "D",
  "exp": "A trigger is a special stored procedure that the database automatically fires in response to a defined data event (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on a table -- it executes as an automatic side-effect, never called directly by the user. Commit (A) is a transaction-control statement that ends a transaction, not a procedure. A Function (B) is explicitly invoked by name/expression, not fired implicitly as a side-effect. 'Alert' (C) is not a real database object at all, just a plausible-sounding distractor.",
  "prov": "Official MPSC final answer key",
