  "exp": "In OSI terminology, the physical and data link layers operate only hop-by-hop (node-to-adjacent-node) across a single physical link, not across the whole path to the final destination. The network layer is the lowest layer whose addressing and routing genuinely span from the original source host all the way to the final destination host (end-to-end delivery of packets), even though it does this through multiple hops. The transport layer (d) also provides end-to-end (process-to-process) service, but it sits above the network layer, so it is not the \"lowest\" layer offering end-to-end semantics. This question is a known point of debate in textbooks, hence lower confidence.",
