  "sub": "Introduction to SQL",
  "q": "Which of the join operations do not preserve non matched tuples?",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Left outer join",
   "B": "Right outer join",
   "C": "Inner join",
   "D": "Natural join"
  },
  "ans": "C",
  "exp": "An inner join returns only rows that have a match in both tables, discarding any unmatched (non-matching) tuples entirely. Left and right outer joins are specifically designed to preserve unmatched rows from one side, padding the missing side with NULLs, so (A) and (B) are the opposite of what is asked. Note that a natural join (D) is technically also a form of equi/inner join and likewise drops unmatched tuples, which makes this question ambiguous between (C) and (D); (C) is chosen as the more general, textbook-standard answer contrasted against the two outer-join options listed.",
  "prov": "Two independent solvers agreed (no official key for this sitting)",
