  "q": "A relational database system needs to maintain data about the relations, such as the schema of the relations. This is called:",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Metadata",
   "B": "Catalog",
   "C": "Log",
   "D": "Dictionary"
  },
  "ans": "B",
  "exp": "DBMSs store descriptive information about the database's own structure (relation schemas, indexes, constraints, etc.) in a dedicated internal store commonly called the system catalog, which the SQL standard exposes via INFORMATION_SCHEMA. 'Metadata' (A) is the correct general category (data about data) but the question asks what this store is specifically 'called', pointing to the more precise term catalog. A transaction log (C) records changes for recovery, not schema information, and while 'data dictionary' (D) is a near-synonym for catalog in many texts, 'catalog' is the term most directly tied to relational-model schema storage.",
