  "sub": "Control Structure Testing",
  "q": "What is Cyclomatic complexity?",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Black box testing",
   "B": "White box testing",
   "C": "Yellow box testing",
   "D": "Green box testing"
  },
  "ans": "B",
  "exp": "Cyclomatic complexity is a white-box (structural) testing metric introduced by Thomas McCabe that counts the number of linearly independent paths through a program's control flow graph, computed as E - N + 2 (edges minus nodes plus 2), used to decide the minimum number of test cases needed for path coverage. It has nothing to do with black-box testing, which examines behavior without looking at internal code structure; 'yellow box' and 'green box' testing are not real, recognized testing categories and are distractors.",
  "prov": "Two independent solvers agreed (no official key for this sitting)",
