  "ans": "A",
  "exp": "Fault-based testing, in the context of object-oriented systems, is specifically defined as designing test cases targeted at plausible faults (e.g. incorrect operator use, wrong message calls, unexpected class interactions) that are likely to actually occur, rather than exhaustively testing every path. Integration testing (B) is about combining and testing modules together, use-based testing (C) tests classes based on how much other classes depend on them, and scenario-based testing (D) exercises realistic usage scenarios -- none of these are defined specifically around targeting 'plausible bugs' the way fault-based testing is.",
