  "sub": "Posets, lattices and mathematical induction",
  "q": "A lattice (L, ∨, ∧) is called distributive if, for all a, b, c ∈ L, it satisfies:",
  "opts": {
   "A": "a ∨ (b ∧ c) = (a ∨ b) ∧ (a ∨ c)",
   "B": "a ∨ b = b ∨ a (commutativity) only",
   "C": "a ∧ a = a (idempotency) only",
   "D": "a ∨ (a ∧ b) = a (absorption) only"
  },
  "ans": "A",
  "exp": "Distributivity is an extra, not-always-true property: a ∨ (b ∧ c) = (a ∨ b) ∧ (a ∨ c), and equivalently a ∧ (b ∨ c) = (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c). Options B, C and D (commutativity, idempotency, absorption) are identities that hold in every lattice by definition, so they can't be used to distinguish a distributive lattice from a non-distributive one. The classic non-distributive examples are the pentagon lattice N5 and the diamond lattice M3.",
  "prov": "Authored for MPSC Informatics Officer 2026 syllabus",
