  "sub": "Disk scheduling (SCAN)",
  "q": "Consider the Work Queue: 23, 89, 132, 42, 187; there are 200 cylinders numbered from 0 - 199; and the disk-head stars at number 100. Compute the total time using SCAN algorithm going towards 0.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "SCAN, the elevator algorithm, moves the disk arm steadily in one direction servicing every request it passes, continues to the end of the disk, then reverses and services the requests lying in the other direction on the return sweep.\n\nGiven: 200 cylinders numbered 0-199, head at cylinder 100, moving toward 0. Pending requests: 23, 89, 132, 42, 187.\n\nMoving inward from 100 the head meets 89, then 42, then 23. Standard SCAN then continues on to cylinder 0 — the physical end of the disk — before reversing; it does not turn around at the last request. (Turning at the last request is the LOOK variant, not SCAN.) It then sweeps outward and services 132, then 187.\n\nService order: 89, 42, 23, (0), 132, 187.\n\nTotal head movement:\n\nInward leg: 100 - 0 = 100 cylinders\n\nOutward leg: 187 - 0 = 187 cylinders\n\nTotal = 100 + 187 = 287 cylinders.\n\nThe question asks for \"total time\", but no seek rate, rotational latency or transfer time is printed with it, so the answer can only be given as total head movement — 287 cylinders. If a per-cylinder seek time t were supplied, total seek time = 287t.\n\nFor comparison, LOOK on the same queue reverses at 23 and gives (100 - 23) + (187 - 23) = 77 + 164 = 241 cylinders.",
  "points": [
   "States the SCAN service order: 89, 42, 23, then 0, then 132, 187",
   "States explicitly that standard SCAN travels to cylinder 0 before reversing, and that stopping at 23 would be LOOK",
   "Shows the sum: inward 100 - 0 = 100, outward 187 - 0 = 187",
   "Gives total head movement = 287 cylinders",
   "Names what is missing: no seek rate is printed, so 'time' can only be expressed as head movement (287t if t per cylinder given)",
   "Optional comparison: LOOK gives 77 + 164 = 241 cylinders"
  ],
