  "sub": "Round Robin scheduling",
  "q": "Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of Round Robin Scheduling.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Round Robin (RR) is preemptive FCFS with a fixed time quantum q. The ready queue is circular; each process gets at most q of CPU time, then is preempted and put at the tail. With n ready processes and quantum q, no process waits more than (n-1)q before its next turn, so response time is bounded.\n\nAdvantages: it is starvation-free, since every process is guaranteed the CPU within (n-1)q; it gives good response time for short, interactive jobs, which is why it is the classic time-sharing algorithm; it is simple to implement (one queue plus a timer interrupt) and needs no advance knowledge of burst lengths, unlike SJF; and it is fair, since all processes of equal priority get equal CPU share.\n\nDisadvantages: average turnaround and waiting time are worse than SJF, because long jobs are repeatedly chopped up; performance depends critically on q. If q is too large, RR degenerates into FCFS; if q is too small, context-switch overhead dominates and throughput collapses. It also ignores priority and process importance, and does not favour I/O-bound processes, which use only a fraction of their quantum.",
  "points": [
   "Defines RR as preemptive FCFS with a fixed time quantum and a circular ready queue",
   "States the response-time bound: no process waits more than (n-1)q for the CPU",
   "Advantages: starvation-free, fair, good response time for interactive jobs, simple, needs no burst-length prediction",
   "Disadvantages: higher average turnaround/waiting time than SJF; ignores priorities",
   "Performance hinges on q: large q degenerates to FCFS, small q means context-switch overhead dominates",
   "Rule of thumb: q should be large compared with context-switch time, and 80% of bursts should be shorter than q"
  ],
  "exp": "",
  "conf": "high",
  "prov": "Jr. Grade of M.E.S. (P&E Dept), August 2018, Paper II, Section B Q20; conventional short-answer question (5 marks) - model answer written for this app; no official MPSC answer exists for it",
  "note": "Model answer — only the Paper-I-syllabus questions from this paper’s Section B were imported (9 of 20)."
