  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "F = (A ⊕ B)'·(C ⊕ D). NOR is a universal gate, so build the two exclusive-OR blocks first and merge them with De Morgan's law: X·Y = (X' + Y')' = NOR(X', Y'). That means the final NOR must be fed with the complements of the two factors, i.e. with (A ⊕ B) and with (C ⊕ D)'.\n\nStage 1 — XOR(A,B), five NOR gates:\nG1 = NOR(A, B)\nG2 = NOR(A, G1)\nG3 = NOR(B, G1)\nG4 = NOR(G2, G3) = (A ⊕ B)'\nG5 = NOR(G4, G4) = A ⊕ B\n\nStage 2 — XNOR(C,D), four NOR gates (the fifth inverter is not needed, because the complement is exactly what the last gate wants):\nG6 = NOR(C, D)\nG7 = NOR(C, G6)\nG8 = NOR(D, G6)\nG9 = NOR(G7, G8) = (C ⊕ D)'\n\nStage 3 — the AND, one NOR gate:\nG10 = NOR(G5, G9) = G5'·G9' = (A ⊕ B)'·(C ⊕ D) = F\n\nTen two-input NOR gates, five gate levels deep. Check A = B = 0, C = 0, D = 1: G4 = 1 so G5 = 0; G9 = (0 ⊕ 1)' = 0; G10 = NOR(0,0) = 1, and (0 ⊕ 0)'(0 ⊕ 1) = 1·1 = 1.",
