  "paper": "TECH1",
  "unit": "2",
  "sub": "Memory hierarchy levels",
  "q": "What is two level memory access in multilevel memory hierarchy?",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "A memory hierarchy is analysed two levels at a time: a small fast level M1 (cache, access time T1) backed by a larger slower level M2 (main memory, T2), where everything in M1 is also present in M2. 'Two-level memory access' is the model giving the average access time the CPU actually sees from that pair. The same formula is then applied repeatedly — registers/cache, cache/main memory, main memory/disk — to cover the whole hierarchy.\n\nLet H be the hit ratio, the fraction of references satisfied by M1. On a hit the cost is T1. On a miss, M1 is searched first and only then is the block fetched from M2, so the cost is T1 + T2. Hence\n\nT_avg = H·T1 + (1 − H)·(T1 + T2)\n\nWorked example, T1 = 10 ns, T2 = 100 ns, H = 0.95:\nT_avg = 0.95 × 10 + 0.05 × (10 + 100) = 9.5 + 5.5 = 15 ns\n\nClose to the 10 ns of the fast level at nearly the cost per bit of the slow one — the point of the hierarchy — and it works only because locality of reference keeps H near 1. At H = 0.5 the same numbers give 5 + 55 = 60 ns.\n\nConvention: this is Stallings' hierarchical (sequential) form. Texts assuming M1 and M2 are probed simultaneously write T_avg = H·T1 + (1 − H)·T2, giving 14.5 ns here. State which you assume.",
  "points": [
   "Defines the two-level pair M1/M2 with access times T1, T2 and inclusion of M1's contents in M2",
   "Defines hit ratio H and gives T_avg = H·T1 + (1 − H)·(T1 + T2)",
   "Worked numbers: T1 = 10 ns, T2 = 100 ns, H = 0.95 gives 9.5 + 5.5 = 15 ns",
   "States explicitly which convention is used, and gives the simultaneous-access variant H·T1 + (1−H)·T2 = 14.5 ns",
   "Explains that locality of reference is what keeps H near 1 (at H = 0.5 the average degrades to 60 ns)",
   "Notes the model is applied recursively across the whole hierarchy"
  ],
  "exp": "",
  "conf": "medium",
  "prov": "Jr. Grade of MES, P&E Cadre (Electrical Wing), July 2023, Paper I, Section B Q12; conventional short-answer question (5 marks) - model answer written for this app; no official MPSC answer exists for it",
  "note": "Model answer"
