  "sub": "Shift and rotate operations",
  "q": "Most of the machines provide a variety of shifting and rotating functions. List out the possible shift and rotate functions with suitable diagrams.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Shifts and rotates displace a register's bits sideways; they differ in what enters the vacated end and what becomes of the bit pushed out. Take an 8-bit register R = 1001 0110 (b7...b0) with carry C = 0, shifted one place.\n\nLogical shift left (SHL): 0 enters b0, b7 goes out to C. R = 0010 1100, C = 1. Multiplies by 2.\nLogical shift right (SHR): 0 enters b7, b0 goes out to C. R = 0100 1011, C = 0. Unsigned divide by 2.\nArithmetic shift left (ASL): same bit movement as SHL; overflow flagged if the sign bit changes.\nArithmetic shift right (ASR): the sign bit is replicated into b7, b0 goes out to C. R = 1100 1011. Signed divide by 2.\nRotate left (ROL): b7 wraps round into b0 and is copied to C. R = 0010 1101.\nRotate right (ROR): b0 wraps round into b7 and is copied to C. R = 0100 1011.\nRotate left through carry (RCL): a 9-bit loop — C enters b0, b7 goes to C. R = 0010 1100, C = 1.\nRotate right through carry (RCR): C enters b7, b0 goes to C. R = 0100 1011, C = 0.\n\nRotates are reversible, losing no bit. Shifts discard the ejected bit, keeping only a copy in the carry flag.",
  "points": [
   "Lists all eight operations: logical/arithmetic shift left and right, rotate left/right, rotate left/right through carry",
   "States for each what fills the vacated bit position and where the ejected bit goes (carry)",
   "Arithmetic shift right replicates the sign bit; arithmetic shift left is the same as logical left but flags sign-change overflow",
   "Traces a concrete 8-bit example (1001 0110, C = 0) through each operation with the resulting pattern and carry",
   "Notes shifts as multiply/divide by 2 and that rotates preserve all bits while shifts lose the ejected bit"
  ],
  "exp": "",
