  "q": "Analyze the time complexity of Prim's Algorithm.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Prim's algorithm grows one tree: over V-1 iterations it repeatedly adds the minimum-weight edge crossing from the tree to a vertex outside it. The cost therefore depends entirely on how extract-min and decrease-key are implemented.\n\nAdjacency matrix with a linear scan of key[]. Each of the V iterations scans all V vertices to find the minimum key, which is O(V), and then relaxes the chosen vertex's whole matrix row, another O(V). Total = V * (V + V) = O(V^2) time. Space is O(V) for key[], parent[] and the in-tree flags, on top of the O(V^2) matrix itself.\n\nAdjacency list with a binary min-heap. Building the heap is O(V). There are V extract-min operations at O(log V) each, and each of the E edges is examined and may cause one decrease-key at O(log V). Total = O(V log V + E log V) = O(E log V) for a connected graph, since E >= V-1.\n\nWhich to prefer: in a dense graph E is theta(V^2), so the heap version degrades to O(V^2 log V) - worse than the matrix version, so use O(V^2) there. In a sparse graph (E much less than V^2) O(E log V) wins clearly. A Fibonacci heap improves the bound further to O(E + V log V).",
  "points": [
   "States the structure of the algorithm: V-1 iterations, each an extract-min plus edge relaxations",
   "Adjacency matrix + linear key[] scan: V iterations x (O(V) scan + O(V) row relax) = O(V^2)",
   "Adjacency list + binary min-heap: V extract-mins at O(log V) plus up to E decrease-keys at O(log V) = O(E log V)",
   "Justifies collapsing O(V log V + E log V) to O(E log V) using E >= V-1 for a connected graph",
   "Says the matrix O(V^2) version is preferable for dense graphs (E ~ V^2, where the heap gives O(V^2 log V)) and the heap version for sparse graphs",
   "Mentions the Fibonacci-heap bound O(E + V log V)"
  ],
  "exp": "",
  "conf": "high",
  "prov": "Jr. Grade of MES, P&E Cadre (Electrical Wing), July 2023, Paper I, Section B Q18; conventional short-answer question (5 marks) - model answer written for this app; no official MPSC answer exists for it",
  "note": "Model answer"
