  "ans": "",
  "model": "Assume context-switch time is zero (the question gives none) and that the three I/O operations genuinely overlap, so no process waits for a device.\n\nEach process repeats a cycle of 20 ms CPU followed by 80 ms I/O, i.e. a 100 ms period in which it needs only 20 ms of CPU. With a 10 ms quantum, a 20 ms burst is simply served as two quanta; the quantum splits the burst but does not change the total CPU work.\n\nTotal CPU demand of the three processes = 3 x 20 ms per 100 ms = 60 ms per 100 ms. Since 60 ms < 100 ms, the single CPU has spare capacity and can serve every request, so no process's cycle is stretched by queueing in the long run.\n\nTimeline sketch (first round): P1 0-10, P2 10-20, P3 20-30, P1 30-40 (burst done, I/O to 120), P2 40-50 (I/O to 130), P3 50-60 (I/O to 140); CPU idle 60-120. Busy 60 of every 100 ms.\n\nCPU utilization = 60/100 = 60%.",
