  "q": "Consider the scenario where disk requests arrive in the order of 82, 170, 43, 140, 24, 16, and 190, with the current head position pointing at 50, determine the total seek time using the SCAN Disk scheduling algorithm.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "The question does not state the direction the head is moving, the number of cylinders on the disk, or the seek time per cylinder, so the answer must be given as total head movement in cylinders. I assume the standard textbook setup: a 200-cylinder disk (0-199) with the head at 50 moving towards higher cylinder numbers.\n\nSCAN (the elevator algorithm) services every request in the current direction up to the end of the disk, then reverses and services the rest.\n\nService order: 82, 140, 170, 190, 199 (end of disk, no request there), then 43, 24, 16.\n\nTotal head movement = (199 - 50) + (199 - 16) = 149 + 183 = 332 cylinders.\n\nIf instead the head is moving towards cylinder 0, the order is 43, 24, 16, 0, then 82, 140, 170, 190, giving (50 - 0) + (190 - 0) = 50 + 190 = 240 cylinders. If the LOOK variant is intended (reverse at the last request, not at the disk edge), the totals are 314 and 208 respectively.\n\nTo convert to seek time, multiply by the given seek rate in ms per cylinder; no rate is printed here.",
