  "sub": "Threads vs processes",
  "q": "What is a thread? Why is it that threads are faster to create than processes?",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "A thread (or lightweight process) is the basic unit of CPU utilization: a single sequential flow of control within a process. It has its own thread ID, program counter, register set and stack, but it shares the process's code section, data section, heap and open OS resources (files, signal handlers) with the other threads of the same process. A traditional process is a single-threaded process; a multithreaded process has several such flows running over one address space.\n\nThreads are cheaper to create than processes because process creation duplicates context while thread creation only adds context:\n\nCreating a process requires allocating a new PCB, building a fresh address space with its own page table, copying or copy-on-write mapping the parent's data, heap and stack, and duplicating the open-file table and other resource descriptors. Creating a thread requires only a TCB, a register set and a new stack, with the address space, page table and resource tables reused as they are.\n\nFor the same reason context switching between threads of one process is cheaper: the memory map does not change, so the TLB and page tables need not be flushed or reloaded.",
  "points": [
   "Defines a thread as the basic unit of CPU utilization with its own thread ID, PC, registers and stack",
   "States what is shared: code, data, heap, open files and signals of the parent process",
   "Process creation cost: new PCB, new address space and page table, copying data/heap/stack and the open-file table",
   "Thread creation cost: only a TCB, register set and stack; address space and resource tables are reused",
   "Adds that thread switching avoids a page-table/TLB flush, so switching is also cheaper"
  ],
  "exp": "",
  "conf": "high",
  "prov": "Jr. Grade of MES, P&E Cadre (Electrical Wing), July 2023, Paper II, Section B Q5; conventional short-answer question (5 marks) - model answer written for this app; no official MPSC answer exists for it",
