  "q": "What are the Structures used in File-System Implementation?",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "File-system implementation uses two families of structures: those stored on disk, which persist, and those held in memory, which exist only while the file system is mounted and speed up access.\n\nOn-disk structures: the boot control block (per volume, first block; holds the boot loader, called the boot block in UFS and the partition boot sector in NTFS); the volume control block (per volume; holds the number of blocks, block size, free-block count and free-block/FCB pointers, called the superblock in UFS and the master file table in NTFS); a directory structure per file system, which maps file names to their FCB numbers; and the file control block (FCB) per file, holding permissions, owner, size, dates and the pointers to the file's data blocks, called the inode in UNIX.\n\nIn-memory structures: the mount table, holding one entry per mounted volume; the directory-structure cache of recently accessed directories; the system-wide open-file table, holding a copy of the FCB of each open file plus an open count; the per-process open-file table, whose entries point into the system-wide table; and buffers holding disk blocks in transit.",
  "points": [
   "Splits the answer into on-disk (persistent) and in-memory (mount-time) structures",
   "On-disk: boot control block, volume control block/superblock, directory structure, per-file FCB",
   "Names the equivalents: superblock (UFS), MFT (NTFS), inode (UNIX FCB)",
   "In-memory: mount table, directory-structure cache, system-wide open-file table, per-process open-file table, buffers",
   "Notes the per-process table entry points into the system-wide table, which holds the FCB copy and open count"
  ],
