  "sub": "Demand paging performance",
  "q": "Suppose the time to service a page fault is on the average 10 milliseconds, while a memory access takes 1 microsecond. Then a 99.99% hit ratio results in average memory access time",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Let p be the page-fault rate. The effective (average) memory access time under demand paging is\n\nEAT = (1 - p) x (memory access time) + p x (page-fault service time).\n\nGiven: memory access time = 1 microsecond, page-fault service time = 10 ms, hit ratio = 99.99%, so p = 1 - 0.9999 = 0.0001 (one fault in every 10,000 references).\n\nUnit conversion first: 10 ms = 10 x 1000 microseconds = 10,000 microseconds.\n\nEAT = 0.9999 x 1 + 0.0001 x 10,000\n    = 0.9999 + 1.0\n    = 1.9999 microseconds, i.e. about 2 microseconds.\n\nSo even a 99.99% hit ratio roughly doubles the average access time: the system runs at about half the speed it would with no paging, because the 10 ms fault costs 10,000 times a memory access. The 0.0001 x 10,000 term contributing a full microsecond is the point of the question, and it shows why demand-paging systems need fault rates far below one in 10,000 to keep the slowdown under 10%.",
  "points": [
   "States EAT = (1 - p) x memory access time + p x page-fault service time",
   "Identifies p = 1 - 0.9999 = 0.0001",
   "Converts 10 ms = 10,000 microseconds",
   "Computes 0.9999 x 1 + 0.0001 x 10,000 = 0.9999 + 1 = 1.9999 microseconds (about 2 microseconds)",
   "Interprets the result: access time is roughly doubled, i.e. about 100% slowdown, despite a 99.99% hit ratio"
  ],
